Transhumanism refers to a way of thinking, even a militant one, that aims for the evolution of the human species through scientific progress, and in particular the development of biotechnologies (Ferry, 2016; Hautebert, 2019; Hottois, 2017). Closely related to transhumanism is posthumanism , which is its radicalized version, insofar as its ultimate goal is the transcendence, and not just the evolution, of the human species. These terms, especially the first, are increasingly prevalent in common parlance, unlike the term " cultism ." However, there is an expression associated with the latter that dominates it, just as it dominates the other two in usage: that of a cult object. A cult film, book, or series is such by virtue of its reception—properly speaking, its cult-like nature . This reception is characterized by a particular passion, a specific enthusiasm, that unites a community of fans (Le Guern, 2002). Thus, everyone agrees that Blade Runner , Ridley Scott's 1982 science fiction film, is a cult classic. So much so that when Denis Villeneuve's sequel was released thirty-five years later, the question was less about the film's quality than whether it would achieve the same cult status as its predecessor. A troubling question indeed: because Blade Runner had initially been a box office failure, the success of Villeneuve's film could jeopardize its cult status, which, moreover, was perhaps already compromised by its status as a sequel (success cannot be commanded, or else it has already arrived)... But in the following pages, another question will be posed: that of the conjunction of cultism and transhumanism. How is it that so many films addressing the theme of transhumanism have attracted a cult following? Isn't it, if not a recipe, at least a rule to be sought, and a story to be reconstructed? To answer this, we will focus on another cult film with a transhumanist theme besides Blade Runner: Ghost in the Shell , whose live-action adaptation, like Villeneuve's film, dates from 2017. The reception of this adaptation, even more than the film itself, constitutes a privileged area of ​​investigation because it allows us to highlight certain traits of the cult audience, such as intellectualism and exoticism. Before analyzing this film and its reception, however, we must strive to better define the phenomenon of cultism.

Cult films and cult movies
Because cult following is generational, it can fuel both forgetfulness—  Casablanca (1942) was a cult film, but it no longer is for younger generations—and nostalgia. Even within a generation, a cult film is more selective in its audience than a film that could be considered mainstream. In other words, the cult community is smaller than the general public. It can even claim a form of marginality. John R. R. Tolkien's The Lord of the Rings (1954-55) was undeniably a cult book until Peter Jackson's film adaptation in the early 2000s turned it into a mainstream phenomenon. To understand this phenomenon, we must go back to its origins (Mathijs & Mendik, 2008; Mathijs & Sexton, 2011 for cult films). In the history of cultism, two periods stand out: the interwar years and the 1970s. The first is when the expression " cult film" originated. The 1920s and 1930s were marked, in intellectual circles, by criticism of the culture industry. A number of thinkers, notably Siegfried Kracauer (2008), questioned the encounter between the masses and a popular culture for which the cinema screen was the primary vehicle. The word " cult " emerged to describe this enthusiasm they observed in movie theaters because this era of criticism of the culture industry was also the period in which the sociology of religion developed. In 1932, the American sociologist Howard Becker edited and translated Leopold von Wiese's Relational Sociology from German, in which " cult " appeared to designate a certain type of religious group. That same year, film critic Harry Alan Potamkin published an article entitled "The Cult of Films." Potamkin, an intellectual from Philadelphia, decided to write about cinema after a trip to Paris in 1925, where he witnessed the fervor surrounding films, particularly American ones. In his article, he observes that this "cult" attracted not only a popular audience, but also writers and artists, such as Philippe Soupault, whom he quotes: "The cult of cinema was born in France. [...] The super-refined boys of the salons found their paradise in William S. Hart and the smile of Pearl White, 'that almost ferocious smile heralding the upheavals of the new world.'" (2020, p. 256) Potamkin thus attributes a French origin to cinematic cultism. A man of the left who believed in cinema for the people, he nevertheless held a critical view of the phenomenon. For him, cultists are young snobs who have a naive, i.e. exoticizing, view of the United States.

It should be noted that at the time, the expression "cult film ," meaning " cult movie , " was not in common use. It would take several more decades for it to enter common usage. In 1970, Andrew Sarris, another American film critic, titled a collection of his articles * Confessions of a Cultist *. At the beginning of this book, he presents himself as a self-proclaimed "cultist." "In the realm of pretense, I stopped bowing my head before the epithet 'cultist' as soon as I realized that the quasi-religious connotation of the term was rather justified for those of us who loved movies beyond reason," he writes (1970, p. 13, my translation). Sarris also contrasts "cultism" with "careerism." He positioned himself as a marginal intellectual in relation to the academics who held positions within the institution, although he himself would eventually become a professor at Columbia University. This is the second decisive moment: in the following years, the phenomenon of midnight movies emerged in the United States, first in New York, around Greenwich Village, and then in the suburbs ( Hoberman & Rosenbaum, 1991). Midnight movies are films shown in theaters at midnight because they possess characteristics that make them suitable for screening at this late hour. They are often small productions with an aesthetic far removed from Hollywood conventions, and whose subject matter is often transgressive. They are either horror films or films with politically subversive content. These films are successful precisely because of their late-night screenings, because they appeal to a different audience than the evening audience—younger and with a countercultural sensibility. Midnight movies were the first cult films, meaning they were the first to be designated as such in a non-anachronistic way. From this starting point, the expression spread widely, encompassing films other than midnight movies , and even objects other than films. The expression traveled beyond Anglo-American geographical and linguistic boundaries, while retaining traces of its origin. In French, the expression " film culte ," with its appositive construction, is clearly an Anglicism. Meanwhile, other languages ​​readily use the English word " cult ." Cults are everywhere, and like everything that is everywhere, they eventually become nowhere. Since the 1980s, the expression has followed a slow but steady path toward commonplace usage, as its contemporary usage clearly demonstrates.

In this exploration, however, we must pause to consider a seminal article, published by Umberto Eco in 1985 entitled "Cult Films and Intertextual Collage." This text is marked by two key contemporary issues. On the one hand, the development of literary theory since the 1960s, from which the notion of intertextuality originates. On the other hand, the advent, in the 1980s, of mainstream cinema where the role of irony becomes more prominent, for example in the films of Steven Spielberg, which feature a quotational, even self-quotational, component. For Eco, it is precisely through its excessive stereotyping that the cult film is defined.

Two photographs make us laugh, but a hundred more move us because we subtly sense that the photographs are conversing with one another and celebrating a reunion. Just as extreme pain meets sensual pleasure, and extreme perversion borders on mystical energy, extreme banality allows us to glimpse the Sublime. No one could have intentionally achieved such a cosmic result. Here, nature has spoken in place of humankind. It is a phenomenon that deserves at least to be revered. (Mathijs & Mendik, 2008, p. 74, my translation)

Eco's theory is based on the analysis of a cult film, Casablanca (anachronistically defined as such since it was released in 1942, but it must be said that its reception spans several generations of viewers). Both the analysis and the theory that stemmed from it have been the subject of criticism (Telotte, 1991; Gabbard & Gabbard, 1999). A twofold reorientation will be proposed here. Eco's perspective may indeed seem too formalistic, leading him to forget that Casablanca is not merely a "collage" but also tells a story that clearly resonated with audiences. The film recounts the reunion of two former lovers who lost touch during the Second World War. The two protagonists meet again in Casablanca, Morocco, at the moment when the woman, played by Ingrid Bergman, must help her husband, a member of the Resistance, escape the Nazis. To achieve her goal, she must turn to her former lover, played by Humphrey Bogart, whom she had abandoned but who is now the only one who can truly help them. He ultimately does so, though he must overcome his bitterness as a rejected lover. It is therefore important to note the presence, in the background, of a historical catastrophe—in this case, World War II—and, in the foreground, the romantic relationship between the characters portrayed by Bergman and Bogart.

However, it is generally a part of a cult film that enters the collective memory, for example, a scene with a strong emotional impact. In this case, it is the farewell between the characters played by Bergman and Bogart, where the latter nostalgically evokes the memory of their past romance in Paris. This is the famous, and oft-parodied, "We'll always have Paris." Eco touches on this point of fragmentation, as we will see later, but without commenting on this scene as he should. Yet, highlighting these two characteristics—the indirect evocation, in the background or fictionalized, of a historical catastrophe, and the emotional force of a scene that predisposes it to fragmentation (and parody)—contributes to the intelligibility of the cult film. This does not, however, presume to offer a formula. Let's take another cult film from the 1980s: Star Wars , which is more accurately a science fiction film series, the latest installments of which were released recently, but which owes most of its cult following to the second film, The Empire Strikes Back ( 1980 ). The film's narrative transposes World War II into the world of science fiction, more precisely the subgenre of space opera , adding a fantastical dimension largely inspired by Tolkien. Thus , there is, once again, an evocation of a historical catastrophe (the same one). There is also the personal story of the hero, Luke Skywalker, who must confront an antagonist who turns out to be his own father. This Oedipal dilemma is at the heart of the iconic scene at the end of The Empire Strikes Back , where Darth Vader (almost literally the "Dark Father") reveals the truth about his parentage: "I am your father." In short, there is the grand narrative, the war, and within this grand narrative, the family tragedy, which can also be interpreted as a crisis that the hero must resolve to reach maturity (Paris & Stoecklin, 2012). One last aspect that Star Wars highlights more than Casablanca (but less than Rebel Without a Cause , another example of Hollywood classicism that has become a cult classic): the cult film is a film for teenagers, either because it features young people or because it presents an adolescent theme (in this case, the conflictual confrontation with the father figure). Marginal yet "refined," historicizing yet imaginative, stereotypical yet impactful—such is the cult object. As for the emotion that permeates it, it is essentially that of loss, melancholy, or nostalgia.

Towards Ghost in the Shell
Let's turn to transhumanism. Many films addressing the subject are considered cult classics, and that's no coincidence. Historically, both cultism and transhumanism find their impetus in the cultural revolution of the 1960s, which was a broad questioning of traditional social and moral frameworks and the place of humans and individuals within them. Transhumanism certainly fits into this emancipatory, yet also terrifying, current of thought, because it contains a significant element of negativity. A cult film like The Rocky Horror Picture Show (1975) echoes the hippie or 1968-era theme of sexual liberation, featuring a transvestite character who has successive sexual relations with both members of the same young engaged couple. But it also constitutes a rewriting of Frankenstein, whose Promethean myth is one of the sources of transhumanism. The Rocky Horror Picture Show is the most resounding success among midnight movies . The vogue for these films ended in the late 1970s with the advent and widespread distribution of video cassettes. The video cassette didn't put an end to cult status, but it did move the phenomenon away from movie theaters. Cult films would no longer be those seen in cinemas at midnight, but those discovered (and rewatched) on video. Perhaps the most representative film of this televised cult status is Blade Runner (1982), which was a box office failure before finding success on video. Ridley Scott's film features android characters called replicants who, rebelling against their creator, must be hunted down and eliminated by the police. Blade Runner is notable for its futuristic, dark, and melancholic urban atmosphere, characteristic of the cyberpunk subgenre of science fiction. It was also notable for the replicants' demand to be considered human and, in particular, to have their lifespan extended, which is only a few years. This limitation is perceived as an obstacle to their fulfillment. The transhuman figure presented in the film is far from triumphant. It could unsettle our humanity, raising the specter of replacement by a post-humanity. But it is limited, castrated in more ways than one. We are clearly witnessing the adolescence of transhumanism.

In 1987, RoboCop was released , a major Hollywood production directed by a European filmmaker, Paul Verhoeven, much like Blade Runner . The film was both a popular success upon its theatrical release and a cult classic. It is an auteur blockbuster that blends mainstream entertainment with a more reflective dimension, offering a critique of Reagan-era capitalism. The film tells the story of a police officer brutally murdered by a gang of criminals who is transformed into a cyborg with superhuman strength. Throughout the narrative, RoboCop, who retains his human brain, gradually rediscovers the memories that were erased, and in doing so, both his individuality and his humanity. Like the replicants, Verhoeven's cyborg evokes empathy. During these same years, an ambitious animation industry emerged in Japan, heavily inspired by Western science fiction. A film like Katsuhiro Otomo's Akira (1988) depicts a futuristic Tokyo resembling the cyberpunk Los Angeles of Blade Runner , but also bearing the traumatic memory of the atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki (again, World War II). The character of Akira, who gives his name to the film without being its protagonist, is a child gifted with paranormal powers developed by the military, powers that led to the destruction of Tokyo. He embodies the childlike fantasy of superhuman strength, albeit in a muted way, as the film's main characters are disinherited and rebellious teenagers. Finally, in this list, which is by no means exhaustive, we must consider Ghost in the Shell , another Japanese animated film released in 1995, directed by Mamuro Oshii based on a manga by Masamune Shirow, which has also achieved cult status. Of all the cult films mentioned, this is undoubtedly the one that most fully explores the subject of transhumanism. The film features a cyborg police officer, a female and eroticized Robocop, who at the end of the film agrees to merge with an artificial intelligence calling itself the Puppeteer, a kind of spirit born within the computer network and having acquired complete autonomy. Ghost in the Shell thus imagines a transcendence of humanity through both biomechanics and advanced technology.

However, it is not Oshii's film that will hold our attention, but rather the 2017 Hollywood live-action adaptation of Ghost in the Shell directed by Rupert Sanders. Why this detour? Because analyzing a cult film requires considering its reception. And a film adaptation is based on reception; it reveals something about how the original work was received. Furthermore, the criticisms leveled at this adaptation by journalists , researchers, and fans alike also reveal something about the cult following surrounding the original work. In this respect, the live-action Ghost in the Shell seems particularly symptomatic, both in its faithfulness and its infidelities, in its strengths and its weaknesses. Among the film's qualities, its visual beauty was the first to be highlighted, which may seem anecdotal, but it's true that the skillful use of digital special effects to create a futuristic world and transhuman characters is interesting because it achieves, on a visual level, this association of nature and artifice. The main criticism concerns a simplification and trivialization of the narrative. The 1995 animated film derived much of its value, in the eyes of fans, from its ambition to articulate a philosophical discourse on transhumanism. The title Ghost in the Shell is an allusion to Arthur Koestler's book The Ghost in the Machine , which is a reflection that takes Cartesian dualism as its starting point to consider the discrepancy between the more intellectual part of humanity and its more primitive tendencies, and therefore the difficulties humanity faces in evolving and forming a harmonious community. And the film, even more so than Masamune Shirow's original manga, offers a reflection on human evolution through the story of this fusion between a cyborg and an artificial intelligence. This reflection is enhanced by the choice to slow down the action, thereby giving the film a contemplative dimension undoubtedly inspired by Blade Runner , which already exhibited this characteristic. In this slowed-down action, a significant portion is given to dialogue, which provides elements for philosophical debate. In the 2017 live-action version , all of this disappears. The film is much more fast-paced, and the dialogue is stripped of its more reflective dimension. We are now faced with a typical Hollywood action film .

The film's ending is also changed: there is no longer a fusion between Major Motoko Kusanagi, the cyborg woman, and the puppeteer. In fact, there isn't really a puppeteer anymore. The antagonist is now named Hideo Kuze, and he is a fully embodied being, inspired by the Ghost in the Shell anime series from the 2000s. He is a kind of male counterpart to the heroine, a cyborg like her, but one who has chosen the path of (cyber)crime. In short, he is no longer a virus but a hacker. Kuze's role will be to make the heroine aware of her humanity and individuality. The major discovers that this criminal is actually a resistance fighter who rebelled against his creators and his own transformation into a cyborg. The film's message is thus simplified and trivialized, since this new version of Ghost in the Shell ultimately offers something very similar to Robocop: a cyborg police officer who rediscovers his humanity while hunting down his murderers. This criticism, which is certainly justified, is also symptomatic of the cult following surrounding Ghost in the Shell . As Harry Potamkin said, cultists are "super-refined guys." Intellectualism is one of the components of cultism. Cultists like films that speak to their intellect, and especially those that set them apart from a wider or more popular audience. In other words, there is a distinction between a cult audience and a popular audience . A version of Ghost in the Shell that simplifies and trivializes its message inevitably meets with disapproval from fans of Mamoru Oshii's film. This intellectualism (or even snobbery) is also associated, as in Potamkin's article, with an exoticizing critique of the film's Americanization. The live-action adaptation is criticized for erasing everything that gives the animated film its Japanese charm (Napier, 2008), including its contemplative slowness, which owes much to Blade Runner , and Kenji Kawai's distinctive music, with its chorus in Japanese. The trivialization and Americanization of the film are therefore the main criticisms leveled by cult critics of Ghost in the Shell .

A moment of truth
From this film review, two points will be focused on in particular, in order to delve deeper into the analysis. First, the recreation of iconic scenes. If this adaptation is cult-like, it's precisely because of its desire to meticulously reproduce the film's memorable scenes, for example, the opening scene depicting the creation of the cyborg woman, or the scene where the Major fights a criminal underwater, demonstrating the superior physical abilities conferred by his transhuman body. In its time, Oshii's film was already considered a technical marvel due to the quality of its animation, and the reproduction of these scenes in Sanders' live-action version is both a tribute and a demonstration of mastery. The problem is that these scenes, in a film where the plot has lost much of its substance, appear precisely as copies devoid of the original's relevance. Hence the pun that inevitably arose regarding the emptiness of the American adaptation: "  a shell without the ghost  ." The other key point concerns the heroine's evolution from one film to the next. While the animated film presented a cold, distant character with a fixed gaze, precisely to question her humanity, Sanders' version offers a much more embodied and humanized cyborg woman. This might appear as a departure from Oshii's film, but much less so from Shirow's original manga, which is lighter, more humorous, and whose characters therefore also appear more human. It's worth noting that, in one episode of the manga (Shirow, 2017, pp. 193-232), Motoko Kusanagi has a lover, who is no longer mentioned in the 1995 film. And in the animated series produced in the 2000s, the heroine's past is largely reconstructed. We learn that during her childhood, she nearly died in a plane crash, which led to her transformation into a cyborg. A romantic subplot is also woven in with the character of Kuze, who supposedly knew her during her childhood after surviving the same plane crash. Motoko's character is thus dramatized, a far cry from the intentions of Oshii's film. However, the live-action version goes even further in this direction, presenting an emotional character despite her robotic nature, seeking to understand her past, and lacking in humanity, as shown in a scene where she takes a prostitute into her home to experience what it feels like to touch a human face. Critical reception has been mixed. Some have praised this added dimension of humanity, while others have seen it as something that, once again, betrays the film by trivializing it, or worse, as a Westernized exaltation of individualism far removed from the transindividual transhumanism of Oshii's film (Daliot-Bul, 2019).

Let's pause our review of the criticisms here to take stock and go further. Sanders' live-action film is undoubtedly an imperfect adaptation of the 1995 film, and from the outset, a relatively ill-conceived project. But this failure is paradoxically interesting in that it can help us understand what makes Ghost in the Shell a cult classic. Let's say we ask a fan why they like Oshii's film. They might answer that it's because of the depth or subtlety of its commentary on transhumanism. However, this would then be less an answer to the question of why the film is a cult classic than to the question of why it is successful. Because cult status is not something that can be fully rationalized ("beyond all reason," according to Sarris), and it can even attach itself to objects whose banality should disqualify them in the eyes of the public (Eco). It is therefore possible that the predictable nature of the plot indirectly reveals what constitutes the cult status of Ghost in the Shell . One effect of this predictability is that it allows the viewer to be much less captivated by the unfolding story. Once the plot is telegraphed , it is no longer its thread that is followed, but perhaps the visual quality of the film, and even more certainly the emotional impact of certain scenes. In this regard, the film's structure is unusual, for an action film, in that its central section is interspersed with intimate scenes. Thus, the heroine is first confronted by the antagonist, Hideo Kuze. He reveals to her that they were both unwitting guinea pigs in the scientific experiment that turned them into cyborgs. Then, she goes back to confront the scientist who created her, Dr. Ouelet , a kind of surrogate mother, who tells her she's been lied to about her origins. She then talks to her police partner about her state of mind. Afterward, she speaks to Ouelet again, who gives her the address of her biological mother so they can meet. All of this unfolds almost entirely without any intervening action scenes. The 2017 Ghost in the Shell thus replaces the contemplative slowness of the animated film with this succession of intimate moments, the most successful and original of which is a live-action invention: the encounter between the heroine and her biological mother.

It is possible to define this scene as a "moment of truth," in the sense that Roland Barthes gave to this expression. Barthes was a literary theorist who came very close to a theory of cultism without ever actually using the term. But the similarity is remarkable between what Eco says about cult films in his 1985 article and what Barthes said about literature a few years earlier:

A book can also inspire a cult following even if it is a great work of art. The Three Musketeers and The Divine Comedy are among the cult books. […] I suspect that a cult film, on the contrary, must possess some organic imperfection. I think that in order to transform a work into a cult object, it must be possible to break it down, dislocate it, take it apart so that only parts remain in memory. (Mathjis & Mendik, 2008, p. 68)

What remains of the Book is the quotation (in the broadest sense): the fragment, the relief that is transported elsewhere. The Divine Comedy is: “Abandon all hope, ye who enter here,” etc. (Barthes, 2003, p. 257)

Eco and Barthes use the same example (among others), Dante's Divine Comedy , to illustrate how the reader extracts a particularly striking sentence or scene from a book they love (or "Book," with a capital letter inherited from Mallarmé, but primarily thinking of the novels of Proust and Tolstoy). Barthes calls these fragments of text that remain in the reader's memory "moments of truth" (p. 151 ff.). Moreover, they can also be fragments of film: the scene of Casanova dancing with an automaton in Federico Fellini's Casanova (p. 160). For Barthes, these are moments of truth because they produce an emotional impact on the reader or viewer , even though they do not occupy a central place in the narrative. The moment of truth in Ghost in the Shell is indeed a low point in the action but a high point in the story, as the heroine is confronted by her biological mother. She doesn't know her because her memories have been erased, and she isn't recognized because she has changed bodies and therefore appearances. The major is welcomed by her mother in her apartment, and the latter begins to tell her about her daughter, whom she believes to be dead, even though she is standing right in front of her. During this dialogue, we learn, among other things, that Motoko was a troubled and rebellious teenager, apparently fatherless, who ran away from home and was arrested by the police after campaigning against transhumanism, which had become state-sanctioned in the dystopian world of the fiction. It's an interior scene, characterized by the realism of the setting, which has nothing science-fictional or futuristic about it. Japanese-inspired elements suggest everyday life, as well as the ritual of tea. The actresses' performances are nuanced. The shot-reverse-shot technique effectively highlights the emotions of these two characters, who are connected without recognizing each other. This emotion on their faces is thankfully restrained, as is the subtle music.

Conclusion
What does this intimate scene, which saves the film from banality, tell us? Doesn't it precisely deliver the moral of the transhumanist cult film it's adapting? The transhuman heroine's return to the maternal figure can be seen as a regression (Alemany Navarro, 2020), but it also suggests that the transhumanist imaginary is fundamentally an adolescent one, hyperbolically reproducing the adolescent crisis, with the difficulties young people generally encounter in establishing bonds with their families, finding meaning in their lives, and accepting their own bodies (Le Breton, 2016). If many films that address transhumanism become cult classics, it's certainly for this fundamental reason. This immaturity, however, shouldn't necessarily be viewed negatively, at least not within an artistic context. After all, the imaginary, by definition, is naive, and it is from this naivety that works of art are constituted. As for true transhumanity , will it simply place humanity in a state of perpetual adolescence? Or will it disappoint, like all realities, destined for a prosaic reduction that will forever sever it from cult status? Let us agree here to leave the answers to others, or to the future.
